nodecode-flow is a rewrite of rewanthtammana's rewanthtammana/hermes-flow:
https://github.com/rewanthtammana/hermes-flow
commit 32a0ba9898ac650e5d38b0d22d8222f3d9a44828
MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Hermes Flow contributors (LICENSE)

The rewrite keeps what a person used the original for: read the sessions a
harness already recorded, show each session as an agent with the handoffs and
lineage that led to it and the tools it called (counts, failures, time), print
that as a terminal tree or as a mermaid flowchart, write one self-contained
offline HTML page, and compare a later run with a trusted-paths baseline so a
new handoff or a tool an agent never used stands out. It is Common Lisp now and
reads Nodecode's own event log (sessions, created facts, tool-call and
tool-result facts) through the kernel's session and store API, instead of a
Python command that opened a SQLite state.db read-only.

Left out: the JSONL/JSON export input (the cell reads this machine's log, not
a pasted file), the bundled customer_review and live_interactions examples
that installed a demo plugin and demo skills into a profile and made model
calls, the deterministic process-agent prototype, and the original's
multi-file JavaScript graph page (the cell's page is rendered in Lisp and
fetches nothing). The trusted-paths baseline is the same JSON shape, but its
connections are built from Nodecode's facts: a session's parent is a handoff
when the child was spawned and lineage when it was forked.
